n you change  you embrace topics that tend to be not  so focused on self content anymore and  I'm curious why why change well just to  assure you it may not be permanent  I mean look my life has been led in many  different places and it seemed to me  that I wanted to draw on all those  resources in different novels I mean  actually the ground beneath her feet has  a very large section of it that has to  do with with contemporary India you know  but but that's the novel about leaving  it's a norm it's a novel about the urge  to go elsewhere you know and and so the  characters do in the end make a journey  across the world and I mean our you know  my life has been a journey across the  world it seemed legitimate to try and  draw on that experience in books but I  mean I shouldn't say that but the next I  mean the novel I'm doing now has a lot  to do with India so you know I mean my  books just have always gone back and  forth you know and probably always will  but I'm not in some way some way giving  up on India you know because how could I  I've never yet written the book which  didn't have an Indian character at the  center of it you know even even Fury  which is entirely set in the West and  has relatively little to do with India  except as I've got a small piece of the  back story still has Indian characters  that it's very centered you know Indian  characters in the Diaspora because I'm  very interested in the Indian diaspora I  think it's a really fascinating thing  that's happened the way in which people  from India have spread across the world  and changed you know and I mean I  remember the great Indian photographers  friend of mine went did a survey in  India  of in England rather of Indians of  Indians living in England and their  children and their descendants aw and he  was really I saw him at the end of it  that he was really quite bewildered he  said that you know that in many ways  these were people who he found it  difficult to recognize as being Indian  because their culture had shifted so  much and yet they themselves clearly  thought that they were and felt that  they had every right so to describe  themselves and so I think in that  relationship between India in India and  India elsewhere you know that's there's  a very interesting subject there you  know and I just think that is part  naturally part of my subject but though  don't worry about it I'll be back all  right last question  Oh is the actor you like well I'm mostly  Kashmiri as you may know I mean three of  my four grandparents of Kashmiri and the  fourth one was partly Kashmiri so you  know it's it's it's important to me and  you know my view is increasingly that  the thing that that the first great  leader of Kashmir Sheikh Abdullah said  immediately after partition which was  Kashmir for the Kashmir ease which has  always been the view of a majority of  people in Kashmir ever since and is the  only thing that nobody pays any  attention to you know that that might be  the answer now you know
 

